          %title Contents
          %p Edward Witten is a theoretical physicist at the Institute for Advanced Study.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://www.sns.ias.edu/~witten/"} website
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_Witten"} wikipedia entry
            %li
              %p
                interview by
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hirosi+Ooguri"} Hirosi Ooguri
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/notices/201505/rnoti-p491.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %em> Adventures in Physics and Math
                ,
                %a{:href => "http://www.kyotoprize.org/en/laureates/commemorative_lectures/"} Kyoto Prize lecture
                2014 (
                %a{:href => "http://www.kyotoprize.org/wp/wp-content/uploads/2016/02/30kB_lct_EN.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/WittenKyotoPrizeLecture.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Witten’s work originates in theoretical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            and stands out as making numerous and deep connections between that and mathematical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
          %p
            Insight gained from the study of quantum field theories and specifically those relevant in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            led Witten to mathematical results deep enough to gain him a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fields+medal"} Fields medal
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#FieldMedalWork"} below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            which many people call “Chern-Simons-Witten theory”,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wess-Zumino-Witten+theory"} Wess-Zumino-Witten theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten+genus"} Witten genus
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gromov-Witten+theory"} Gromov-Witten theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Seiberg-Witten+theory"} Seiberg-Witten theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rozansky-Witten+invariant"} Rozansky-Witten invariant
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten+conjecture"} Witten conjecture
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Horava-Witten+theory"} Horava-Witten theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diaconescu-Moore-Witten+anomaly"} Diaconescu-Moore-Witten anomaly
            etc..
          %p
            Despite the deeply theoretical and abstract mathematical aspects of his work, Witten has visibly always been motivated by fundamental questions in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenology
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} and cosmology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            crucially enters into the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experimental
            detection of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+particle"} Higgs particle
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory+results+applied+elsewhere"} string theory results applied elsewhere
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic string theory
            is a plausible candidate for a fundamental
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GUT"} grand unified gauge field theory
            including
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
          %p
            Since about the turn of the millennium Witten has tended to more esoteric mathematical aspects of string theory, such as its relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Khovanov+homology"} Khovanov homology
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+Langlands+duality"} geometric Langlands duality
            which apparently the string theory community at large is following less enthusiastically than it was the case during the excited 1990s.
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Quotes"} Quotes
              %li
                %a{:href => "#FieldMedalWork"} Fields medal work
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_lab_entries"}
                  Related
                  = succeed "Lab" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %h2#Quotes Quotes
          %p
            On the (lack of) understanding of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
          %p
            In
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Witten87EllipticGenera"} Witten 87, very last sentence
          %blockquote
            %p
              A properly developed theory of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+cohomology"} elliptic cohomology
              is likely to shed some light on what
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
              really means.
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -40px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/WittenInDavisBrown_StringMathematicsGeometry.jpg", :width => "420px"}/
          %p
            %br/
          %p#StringTheoryAtItsFinestQuote In an interview recorded (1988) in:
          %ul
            %li#DavisBrown88
              P C W Davis &amp; J Brown (eds.),
              = succeed "," do
                %em Superstrings: A theory of everything?
              = succeed "):" do
                %a{:href => "https://books.google.us/books?id=SrSyTS4kzP8C&lpg=PP1&vq=contents&pg=PA90#v=onepage&q&f=false"} gbooks
          %blockquote
            %p
              String theory at its finest is, or should be, a new branch of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            %p I would consider trying to elucidate this proper generalization of geometry as the central problem of physics, certainly the central problem of string theory. (p. 96)
            %p What should have happened, by rights, is that the correct mathematical structures should have been developed in the twenty-first or twenty-second century, and then finally physicists should have invented string theory as a physical theory that is made possible by these structures. (p. 102)
            %p we are paying the price for the fact that we didn’t come by this thing in the usual way. (p. 103)
          %p
            %br/
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 20px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/WittenInDavisBrown_StringMathematicsGeometryII.jpg", :width => "420px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                From
                %a{:href => "#DavisBrown88"} Davis and Brown 1988
          %p#FurtherInDavisBrown88
            Further in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#DavisBrown88"} Davis &amp; Brown 1988, p. 102
          %blockquote
            %p#BeenSaid
              It’s been said that string theory is part of the physics of the twenty-first century that fell by chance into the twentieth century. That’s a remark that was made by a leading physicist &amp;lbrack;
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniele+Amati"}> Daniele Amati
              , see
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "#Witten21stCenturyQuote"} below
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %a{:href => "#FurtherInDavisBrown88"} This point
            appears again in
          %ul
            %li#Witten02AstronomyIssue
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Can scientists’ “theory of everything” really explain all the weirdness the universe displays?
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://www.ias.edu/sites/default/files/sns/string(3).pdf"} pdf
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Witten_AstronomyIssue2002.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p
            and also in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "http://www.pbs.org/wgbh/nova/elegant/view-witten.html"} Nova interview 2003
          %blockquote
            %p#Witten21stCenturyQuote
              Back in the early ’70s, the Italian physicist,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniele+Amati"} Daniele Amati
              reportedly said that string theory was part of 21st-century physics that fell by chance into the 20th century. I think it was a very wise remark.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            In an interview with Graham Farmelo,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "https://grahamfarmelo.com/the-universe-speaks-in-numbers-interview-5"} “The Universe Speaks in Numbers”, interview 5
          %blockquote
            %p#WhatIsMissing
              I actually believe that
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
              is on the right track toward a deeper explanation. But at a very fundamental level it’s not well understood. And I’m not even confident that we have a good concept of what sort of thing is missing or where to find it. The reason I am not is that in hindsight it is clear the view given in the 1980s of what is missing was too narrow. Instead of discovering what we thought was missing, we broadened the picture in the 90s, in unexpected directions.
          %p
            %br/
          %h2#FieldMedalWork Fields medal work
          %p In
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Atiyah"}> Michael Atiyah
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the work of Edward Witten
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://www.mathunion.org/fileadmin/ICM/Proceedings/ICM1990.1/ICM1990.1.ocr.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://web.archive.org/web/20250520085051/https://www.mathunion.org/fileadmin/ICM/Proceedings/ICM1990.1/ICM1990.1.ocr.pdf"} archive
          %p
            the following “influential papers” are listed as relevant for Edward Witten receiving the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://159.226.47.99:8080/general/prize/medal/1990.htm"} Fields Medal in 1990
          %p First of all
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em> Supersymmetry and Morse theory
                , J. Differential Geom. Volume 17, Number 4 (1982), 661-692. (
                %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.jdg/1214437492"}> Euclid
                )
              %p
                This discusses deformations of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetric+quantum+mechanics"} supersymmetric quantum mechanics
                on a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
                and how its supersymmetric ground states are related to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morse+theory"} Morse theory
                of a deformation function. The way this supersymmetric quantum mechanics appears as the point-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
                limit of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+superstring"} type II superstring
                is explained at the end of
            %li
              %p
                %em> Global anomalies in string theory
                , in W. Bardeen and A. White (eds.)
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Symposium on Anomalies, Geometry
          %p
            which otherwise is on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomalies"} quantum anomalies
            in string theory, such as the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+anomaly"} Green-Schwarz anomaly
          %p Finally Atiyah’s section 2 mentions
          %ul
            %li
              (with
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cumrun+Vafa"} Cumrun Vafa
              = succeed "," do
                %em Eigenvalue inequalities for Fermions in gauge theories
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.cmp/1103941573"} Euclid
          %p Then
          %ul
            %li#Witten87EllipticGenera
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
                ,
                %em Elliptic Genera And Quantum Field Theory
                , Commun.Math.Phys. 109 525 (1987) (
                %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.cmp/1104117076"}> euclid:1104117076
                )
              %p
                on the
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+genus"} elliptic genus
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten+genus"} Witten genus
                as the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
                of the
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+superstring"} type II superstring
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+superstring"} heterotic superstring
          %p
            And various articles on the foundations of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+field+theory"} topological field theory
            such as
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em> Topological quantum field theory
                , Comm. Math. Phys. Volume 117, Number 3 (1988), 353-386 (
                %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.cmp/1104161738"}> Euclid
                )
              %p
                on
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topologically+twisted+D%3D4+super+Yang-Mills+theory"} topologically twisted D=4 super Yang-Mills theory
                and the corresponding twists of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
                which yield the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"} A-model
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-model"} B-model
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+string"} topological string
                2d
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFTs"} TQFTs
            %li
              %p
                %em On the structure of the topological phase of two dimensional gravity
                Nuclear Phys, B 340 (1990) 281 (
                %a{:href => "http://ccdb5fs.kek.jp/cgi-bin/img_index?200033155"}> scans
                )
              %p
                on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+string"} topological string
                limit of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+quantum+gravity"} 2d quantum gravity
                theory of the string, including the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"} A-model
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-model"} B-model
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+twists"} topological twists
                of the
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+superstring"} type II superstring
          %p and
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em> Quantum field theory and the Jones polynomial
                , Comm. Math. Phys, 121 (1989) 351
              %p
                on the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+of+3d+Chern-Simons+theory"} quantization of 3d Chern-Simons theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jones+polynomial"} Jones polynomial
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+invariant"} knot invariant
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
                and its
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographic
                relation to the quantization of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+model"} WZW model
                (the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
                propagating on a suitable
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
          %h2#related_lab_entries
            Related
            = succeed "Lab" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"}> string theory
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory+on+G%E2%82%82-manifolds"} M-theory on G₂-manifolds
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gromov-Witten+invariants"}> Gromov-Witten invariants
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rozansky-Witten+invariant"} Rozansky-Witten invariant
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Seiberg-Witten+theory"}> Seiberg-Witten theory
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Seiberg-Witten+equations"} Seiberg-Witten equations
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Seiberg-Witten+invariant"} Seiberg-Witten invariant
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Seiberg-Witten+flow"} Seiberg-Witten flow
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetric+quantum+mechanics"} supersymmetric quantum mechanics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fuzzy+dark+matter"} fuzzy dark matter
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+gravity"} Chern-Simons gravity
          %p (…)
          %blockquote
            %p clearly, this list deserves be further expanded…
